{"cluster_id": "C-2814", "topic": "server_root_id is a required, validated, immutable", "target": "architecture/mounts-and-leases", "member_ids": ["AUDIT-M-010"], "canonical_claim": "server_root_id is a required, validated, immutable config parameter, not derived from ServerUUID; a superseded writer (whose writer_epoch no longer matches the live mount) issues no mutable CAS/PUT because the write-path lease fence is a local in-memory check with no per-write S3 read, tripping the disk to lost/fail-closed on any supersession.", "issue_ids": [], "sources": ["docs/superpowers/cas/01-architecture.md#incarnation-identity"]}
{"cluster_id": "C-2815", "topic": "decommissionPoolMember's only list-based deletions target three", "target": "architecture/mounts-and-leases", "member_ids": ["AUDIT-M-087"], "canonical_claim": "`decommissionPoolMember`'s only list-based deletions target three physically loose non-catalog debris prefixes rooted at the victim's own server-root subtree \u2014 the manifest sweep, the staging drain (`<pool_prefix>/staging/<victim_srid>/`), and the mountpoint drain (`<pool_prefix>/roots/<victim_srid>/`) \u2014 and none of them participates in namespace-ownership selection.", "issue_ids": ["T3"], "sources": [".superpowers/sdd/2026-08-02-cas-stage-b-remaining/t3-draft-report.md#L248-L257"]}
{"cluster_id": "C-2816", "topic": "For non-atomic bucket-sync backups, gc/server-roots/<id>/epoch (a", "target": "architecture/mounts-and-leases", "member_ids": ["AUDIT-M2-058"], "canonical_claim": "For non-atomic bucket-sync backups, `gc/server-roots/<id>/epoch` (a `WriterEpochMonotoneUnique` durable-monotone counter) can be captured before manifests minted under a later epoch, letting a restored writer re-issue a used `writer_epoch`; the chosen backup design avoids this by never replicating identity objects at all, rather than sequencing the copy to take identity objects last.", "issue_ids": [], "sources": ["docs/superpowers/cas/10-backups.md#backup-assets"]}
{"cluster_id": "C-2817", "topic": "In the CAS mount-fence write-grace plan", "target": "architecture/mounts-and-leases", "member_ids": ["AUDIT-M2-413"], "canonical_claim": "In the CAS mount-fence write-grace plan, no test may `EXPECT_THROW` a `LOGICAL_ERROR`; Part B failure paths must surface as `INVALID_STATE` or `NETWORK_ERROR`, which are safe to assert with `EXPECT_THROW` under sanitizers.", "issue_ids": [], "sources": ["docs/superpowers/plans/2026-07-28-cas-fence-observability-and-write-grace.md#self-review"]}
{"cluster_id": "C-2818", "topic": "The separate process_epoch field is dropped", "target": "architecture/mounts-and-leases", "member_ids": ["B005-006"], "canonical_claim": "The separate `process_epoch` field is dropped because the writable path already sets `process_epoch = writer_epoch`; the merged heartbeat's `writer_epoch` serves both mount and orphan-sweep epoch roles.", "issue_ids": [], "sources": ["docs/superpowers/specs/2026-07-02-cas-gc-ack-floor-fence-redesign.md#merged-server-heartbeat-replaces-mount-object--watermark-object"]}
{"cluster_id": "C-2819", "topic": "For a 10,000-table pool, GC startup", "target": "architecture/mounts-and-leases", "member_ids": ["B006-041"], "canonical_claim": "For a 10,000-table pool, GC startup cost is O(table/ref count): observed root metadata reads were `CasRootList=110255` and `CasRootGet=42127`, about 152,000 S3 operations, while recovery remained 64 seconds because it did not enumerate blobs.", "issue_ids": [], "sources": ["docs/superpowers/worklogs/2026-07-03-scenarios-full-scale-campaign.md#L205-L214"]}
{"cluster_id": "C-2820", "topic": "DEFER does not write gc/state or", "target": "architecture/mounts-and-leases", "member_ids": ["B006-071"], "canonical_claim": "DEFER does not write `gc/state` or advance the round and uses a leader-local in-memory `rounds_since_last_fold`; the lease-renew CAS still runs because it is required to retain leadership and heartbeat liveness.", "issue_ids": [], "sources": ["docs/superpowers/specs/2026-07-06-cas-gc-round-skip-unchanged-design.md#L144-L159"]}
{"cluster_id": "C-2821", "topic": "CAS introspection is read-only and fail-open", "target": "architecture/mounts-and-leases", "member_ids": ["B006-087"], "canonical_claim": "CAS introspection is read-only and fail-open per row: a corrupt or racing mount body becomes a `state='corrupt'` row or is skipped, never an exception into the query; unlike `computeHeartbeatFloor`, `listMounts` performs no fence writes.", "issue_ids": [], "sources": ["docs/superpowers/plans/2026-07-06-cas-introspection-package.md#L5-L20"]}
{"cluster_id": "C-2822", "topic": "The retired-view syncer starts only under", "target": "architecture/mounts-and-leases", "member_ids": ["B006-113"], "canonical_claim": "The retired-view syncer starts only under `background_watermark` after the mount fence is armed and does not restart per remount because it is tied to the Store lifetime rather than a mount incarnation.", "issue_ids": [], "sources": ["docs/superpowers/plans/2026-07-06-cas-lease-view-sync-decouple.md#L497-L510"]}
{"cluster_id": "C-2823", "topic": "The rev.6 direction moves contract", "target": "architecture/mounts-and-leases", "member_ids": ["B014-017", "B014-061", "B015-001", "B034-011", "B043-025"], "canonical_claim": "The rev.6 direction moves ref-protocol exclusivity to the lease boundary: mount takeover waits old-lease expiry plus `T_mat`, with the handover invariant covering all client timeouts plus `T_mat`; the ref protocol can then publish one copy of live state asynchronously without grace-window replay. Rev.6 solves writer exclusivity at the mount-lease boundary and removes the ref protocol's grace machinery; because recovery is lazy per namespace, sealing inside `ensureRefTableRecovered` before exposing table state is equivalent to sealing before writable for `NoDivergentFold`. CAS ref-writer exclusivity is established at the mount-lease boundary, after which the ref protocol is single-writer by construction. The rev.6 ref protocol made writer exclusivity a mount-lease handover property using an unclean-handover wait, a `released_clean` fast path, an eager recovery-snapshot seal, and publish-from-live snapshots; the later v9 in-band `EpochSeal` retired `materialization_grace_ms`/`T_mat`. Mount lease handover uses rev.6 lease-boundary exclusivity: an unclean handover waits, a clean release is fast, and a successor commits an eager recovery-snapshot seal before it becomes writable.", "issue_ids": ["rev.6"], "sources": ["docs/superpowers/cas/BACKLOG.md#ref-protocol", "docs/superpowers/cas/how-we-got-here.md#turn-cursors-and-lease", "docs/superpowers/plans/2026-07-13-cas-ref-lease-exclusivity-rev6.md#rev6-plan", "docs/superpowers/specs/2026-07-13-cas-ref-lease-exclusivity-rev6-design.md#principle", "docs/superpowers/worklogs/2026-07-12-unattended-refsnaplog-stabilization.md#L242-L260"]}

